+/// The compare-and-swap that **repoints** one chunk — rewriting the placement of the
+/// `ChunkRef` in whichever record carries it — with the guarantees the flat repoint has
+/// always had: exactly one writer wins, and a loser's rebuilt fragments are collectable
+/// garbage rather than a corrupted record (`0005:200-203`, ADR-0015).
+///
+/// It takes the chunk's **new placement**, never a replacement [`ChunkRef`]: a repoint is
+/// a *move*, so the chunk's id, EC scheme and length are carried over from the record the
+/// CAS is preconditioned on and cannot be changed through this door at all
+/// ([`repoint_placement`]).
+///
+/// * a flat map's chunk: `require(inode == prior)` + the rewritten root at `version + 1`,
+///   exactly as reconstruction and rebalance have always committed it;
+/// * a segmented map's chunk: `require(seg:<g>:<E>:<i> == prior)` **and**
+///   `require(inode == prior_root)` — the second precondition is what makes the repoint
+///   lose to a concurrent supersede or delete of the generation (`0016:354`, decision
+///   7(f)). The root is not rewritten, so its version does not move: the *segment* record
+///   is the one that changed.
+///
+/// **Every precondition names bytes that were READ, never a re-encoding** — the root's
+/// through [`RootGeneration::prior`], the segment's through [`SegmentHome::prior`]. A
+/// precondition rebuilt with `encode(record)` would hold only while decode→encode is
+/// byte-identical, and where it is not the repoint does not fail loudly: it conflicts
+/// against a value the store never held, every pass, forever.
+///
+/// The caller adds its own mutations — the repair-queue drain, the `orphan:` evidence for
+/// the position it vacated — to the returned batch and commits it. Returning the batch
+/// rather than committing it is what lets a caller assemble the whole move as **one**
+/// version-conditional commit.
+///
+/// **Deliberately NOT here: a destination pre-mark, and a destination drain fence —
+/// deferred: #653.** Decision 7's `seg:` row also asks a repoint to pre-mark
+/// `orphan:<P_new>` before the destination fragment is written, so a lost CAS leaves
+/// evidence for the abandoned copy. That rule's other half is the retirement drain
+/// re-reading each segment's current placement at drain time (`0016:2416-2430`) — the two
+/// only close the race together, and half of it here would write evidence no drain yet
+/// consumes. Both shapes go through this one builder, so either would silently change the
+/// flat path too. Until #653 lands them together, this keeps the flat repoint's
+/// long-standing behaviour unchanged for both shapes, and the callers in this slice write
+/// **nothing at all** before the batch is built — a refused repoint leaves no abandoned
+/// copy to reclaim (`crates/custodian/src/reconstruction.rs`,
+/// `crates/custodian/src/rebalance.rs`).
+pub fn repoint_chunk(
+    inode_id: InodeId,
+    root: &RootGeneration,
+    home: &ChunkHome,
+    placement: Vec<DServerId>,
+) -> Result<WriteBatch> {
+    let root_key = inode_key(inode_id);
+    let prior_root = root.record();
+    match home {
+        ChunkHome::Root { position } => {
+            let mut chunks = prior_root
+                .chunk_map
+                .as_flat()
+                .ok_or(ChunkMapError::MapShapeChanged)?
+                .to_vec();
+            let slot = chunks
+                .get_mut(*position)
+                .ok_or(ChunkMapError::ChunkPositionOutOfRange {
+                    position: *position,
+                })?;
+            repoint_placement(slot, *position, placement)?;
+            let next_root = InodeRecord {
+                chunk_map: ChunkMap::Flat(chunks),
+                version: next_root_version(prior_root)?,
+                // Everything else is carried over unchanged: a placement repair re-commits
+                // the SAME content, so it preserves the object metadata (ADR-0047) and the
+                // state the caller resolved.
+                ..prior_root.clone()
+            };
+            // The bytes the resolve READ, not `encode(prior_root)`: this is the value the
+            // store holds, so the CAS matches whatever encoding it was written under.
+            let prior_bytes = root.prior().clone();
+            let next_bytes = encode(&next_root);
+            // **Charged on GROWTH, not absolutely.** A repoint keeps the placement
+            // vector's length but not its width (a `DServerId` renders in 1 to 20 decimal
+            // digits), so moving a wide-EC chunk onto high-numbered servers grows the
+            // record — and a flat root is bounded by [`MAX_VALUE_BYTES`] and nothing else,
+            // so a large object's root can sit right under it. Absolutely, though, this
+            // would refuse to repair an object whose root *predates* this ceiling
+            // discipline at all — including a move onto narrower ids, which shrinks it —
+            // turning a record one backend would reject into an object no pass can ever
+            // fix. A repoint may not make a record's ceiling problem worse; resolving one
+            // it inherited is not its job. Growth is measured against the bytes the store
+            // actually holds, which is the only length the ceiling is ever charged on.
+            if next_bytes.len() > prior_bytes.len() {
+                check_value_ceiling(&root_key, &next_bytes)?;
+            }
+            Ok(WriteBatch::new()
+                .require(root_key.clone(), prior_bytes)
+                .put(root_key, next_bytes))
+        }
+        ChunkHome::Segment { segment, position } => {
+            // The home and the root must name the SAME generation, and the root must name
+            // this segment. Without both checks a home resolved from a generation that has
+            // since been retired, paired with the *current* root, satisfies every other
+            // precondition this batch carries: `require(seg == prior)` holds against a
+            // retired record the drain has not reached yet, `require(inode == prior_root)`
+            // holds against a root that never named it, and the repoint reports
+            // `Committed` while writing into a record no consumer will ever read — the
+            // repair silently lost, its caller told the fragment moved.
+            let table = prior_root
+                .chunk_map
+                .segmented()
+                .ok_or(ChunkMapError::MapShapeChanged)?;
+            let (nonce, epoch, index) = parse_seg_key(segment.key())?;
+            if nonce != *table.group().nonce() || epoch != table.group().epoch() {
+                return Err(ChunkMapError::SegmentGroupMismatch {
+                    root: format!("{}:{}", table.group().nonce(), table.group().epoch()),
+                    home: format!("{nonce}:{epoch}"),
+                }
+                .into());
+            }
+            if !table.segments().iter().any(|s| s.index == index) {
+                return Err(ChunkMapError::SegmentUnknown {
+                    nonce: nonce.to_string(),
+                    epoch,
+                    index,
+                }
+                .into());
+            }
+            // Through the record's own mutator, which moves the placement and nothing else
+            // ([`SegmentRecord::repoint`]) — decoded from the very bytes the precondition
+            // names, so what is rewritten is exactly what the CAS pins.
+            let mut record = decode_segment_record(index, segment.prior())?;
+            record.repoint(*position, placement)?;
+            let next_bytes = encode(&record);
+            // **A repoint can make a record bigger, so the per-record ceiling is charged
+            // here too.** The publication's headroom is arithmetic about the *chunk*
+            // encoding (`0016:218-232`), not a bound on later repoints, so enough of them
+            // can carry a segment past [`MAX_VALUE_BYTES`]. Handing that batch to the
+            // backend would fail the repair with a rejection no retry and no finer split
+            // can fix; refusing here names the `seg:` key, before anything is written, and
+            // leaves the record exactly as it was.
+            //
+            // The keys are not charged: both this batch names (`inode:<id>` and a
+            // fixed-width `seg:` key) are bounded by their own grammars, so no ceiling
+            // check could bind on them.
+            check_value_ceiling(segment.key(), &next_bytes)?;
+            Ok(WriteBatch::new()
+                .require(segment.key().to_vec(), segment.prior().clone())
+                .require(root_key, root.prior().clone())
+                .put(segment.key().to_vec(), next_bytes))
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The version the record a compare-and-swap over `prior` publishes must carry:
+/// `prior.version + 1`, **checked** ([`ChunkMapError::RootVersionOverflow`]).
+fn next_root_version(prior: &InodeRecord) -> std::result::Result<u64, ChunkMapError> {
+    prior
+        .version
+        .checked_add(1)
+        .ok_or(ChunkMapError::RootVersionOverflow {
+            version: prior.version,
+        })
+}
+
+/// One record's value against [`MAX_VALUE_BYTES`], the value ceiling every backend
+/// inherits (`crates/traits/src/lib.rs:995-999`).
+///
+/// Charged **before** the batch is handed to a store, because a record that crosses the
+/// ceiling is not a capacity cost but a state nothing exits: the CAS that would rewrite it
+/// is `require(key, encode(prior))`, which the backend then rejects permanently, so the
+/// record can never be repaired *or* overwritten (`docs/principles.md` §5 C-1).
+fn check_value_ceiling(key: &[u8], value: &[u8]) -> std::result::Result<(), ChunkMapError> {
+    if value.len() > MAX_VALUE_BYTES {
+        return Err(ChunkMapError::ValueOverCeiling {
+            key: String::from_utf8_lossy(key).into_owned(),
+            bytes: value.len(),
+            ceiling: MAX_VALUE_BYTES,
+        });
+    }
+    Ok(())
+}
